WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 106: Henry Flagler's Over-Sea Railroad to Key West: The Eighth Wonder of the World

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Today, we journey into the dazzling, sun-drenched turquoise waters of the Florida Keys to witness one of the most audacious, monumental, and impossible engineering triumphs in human history: Henry Flagler's Oversea Railroad.

4
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:38.320
[Rebecca] In the early 20th century, 75-year-old standard oil multimillionaire Henry Flagler looked out across 128 miles of open ocean, coral reefs, and mosquito-infested mangrove swamps separating mainland Florida from the island outpost of Key West.

5
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:48.640
[Rebecca] Skeptics labeled it Flagler's Folly, insisting that no railroad could bridge the raging Atlantic Ocean and survive the deadly hurricanes of the Caribbean.

6
00:00:48.640 --> 00:01:02.400
[Rebecca] Over seven grueling years, an army of 40,000 workers, battling three devastating hurricanes that killed hundreds, poured concrete into open sea, building over 17 miles of concrete viaducts and steel spans.

7
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.640
[Rebecca] On January 22nd, 1912, Flagler's train rolled triumphantly into Key West.

8
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, magnificent story.

9
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.160
[George] Rockefeller and Samuel Andrews to found the Standard Oil Company.

10
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:48.480
[George] As the master strategist and financial brain behind Standard Oil's ruthless rebate agreements and pipeline networks, Flagler accumulated a personal fortune that made him one of the wealthiest men on planet Earth.

11
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:57.520
[George] In 1885, at the age of 55, Flagler visited Florida with his ailing first wife, seeking a warm winter climate for her health.

12
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:04.600
[George] He was appalled by the lack of modern infrastructure, comfortable hotels, and reliable transportation in the state.

13
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:12.040
[George] Flagler recognized Florida's immense potential as an agricultural empire and winter paradise for wealthy Americans.

14
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:21.400
[George] Retiring from active management of standard oil, he poured his colossal fortune into the state, purchasing and constructing the Florida East Coast Railway.

15
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:30.840
[George] Year by year, Flagler's tracks pushed southward through the virgin wilderness, transforming sleepy coastal villages into world-class resort cities.

16
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:37.640
[George] He built magnificent Gilded Age luxury hotels, dredged harbors, and laid out the modern cities of St.

17
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.040
[George] Augustine, Daytona Beach, Palm Beach, and Miami.

18
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:55.400
[George] In 1896, when his railroad reached Biscayne Bay, local residents voted to name the new town Flagler, but the modest tycoon declined, insisting they adopt the ancient indigenous name, Miami.

19
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:06.440
[George] Flagler's bold infrastructure projects brought modern refrigeration, telephone lines, and electric street lighting to communities that had previously lived in pioneer isolation.

20
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:16.360
[George] By 1900, Miami was the thriving southern terminus of the Florida East Coast Railway, but Henry Flagler's grand vision was far from complete.

21
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:23.960
[George] 128 miles to the southwest, across a chain of coral islands and open ocean channels, lay Key West.

22
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:36.040
[George] At the time, Key West was the largest and wealthiest city in Florida, bustling with 20,000 residents, thriving cigar factories, sponge fleets, and deep water commercial shipping.

23
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:45.040
[George] Key West was the closest American deepwater port to the Caribbean and Central America, situated just 90 miles from Havana, Cuba.

24
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.240
[George] Then, in 1904, came the geopolitical development that electrified Flagler.

25
00:03:50.560 --> 00:04:00.640
[George] The United States government under President Theodore Roosevelt acquired the Panama Canal Zone and commenced construction on the Great Canal connecting the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

26
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:12.960
[George] Flagler realized that when the Panama Canal opened, all international commercial shipping from Europe and the American East Coast to Central America and the Pacific would pass directly past Key West.

27
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:27.040
[George] If a railroad could connect Key West to the mainland, passengers and freight could travel by rail directly from New York to Key West, transfer to fast steamships to Havana, or load onto freighters bound for the canal in record time.

28
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.240
[George] At 74 years of age, Flagler summoned his chief engineer, Joseph C.

29
00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.560
[George] Carter, pointed to a map of the Florida Keys, and asked, Can you build a railroad across the sea?

30
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.840
[George] Carter replied.

31
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.600
[George] But it will be the most difficult and expensive project ever undertaken by private enterprise.

32
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.800
[George] Flagler smiled and said, Go ahead.

33
00:04:50.800 --> 00:05:01.760
[George] The strategic opening of the Panama Canal promised to revolutionize international trade routes, positioning Florida as the commercial crossroads of the Atlantic and Pacific worlds.

34
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.640
[George] Construction commenced in 1905, under the day-to-day direction of brilliant young civil engineer William J.

35
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.520
[George] Crome.

36
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.200
[George] The logistical challenges were staggering beyond anything ever encountered in railroad engineering.

37
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] The route required building 128 miles of railway from homestead across 43 separate islands and 30 miles of open ocean channels, with tidal currents rushing between the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico at up to eight knots.

38
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:37.640
[George] Furthermore, the quays were an uninhabited wilderness with virtually no fresh water, food, or building materials.

39
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:43.000
[George] Flagler's company had to assemble a gargantuan floating navy of construction equipment.

40
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:52.680
[George] Twelve steamboats, 80 barges, 30 tugboats, 14 floating Derrick cranes, and dozens of floating houseboats to house thousands of workers.

41
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.400
[George] Fresh water was an absolute lifeline.

42
00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:05.800
[George] Over 4.5 million gallons of drinking water were transported every month in specialized tank cars from wells on the Florida mainland and distributed across the floating camps.

43
00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:22.760
[George] To recruit an army of 40,000 laborers over the course of the project, flagler's agents scoured the ports of the Atlantic, hiring European immigrants, American bridge builders, New York dockworkers, and experienced Afro-Caribbean laborers from the Bahamas and Grand Cayman.

44
00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:38.840
[George] Workers lived in wooden bunkhouses on remote coral rocks and aboard floating quarterboats, enduring scorching tropical heat, swarms of mosquitoes so dense they extinguished kerosene lanterns, and dangerous coral infections known as coral poisoning.

45
00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:49.880
[George] Engineers employed innovative subsurface sonar probing and diver reconnaissance to map deep water granite trenches, anchoring massive caissons directly into ancient coral beds.

46
00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:57.720
[George] The greatest enemy of the oversea railroad was not the open ocean, but the catastrophic tropical hurricanes of the Caribbean.

47
00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.120
[George] In October 1906, the first major disaster struck.

48
00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:10.120
[George] A Category III hurricane slammed into the Middle Keys with 120 mile per hour winds and a 12-foot storm surge.

49
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:22.320
[George] Floating quarterboat number 4, anchored off Long Key with 160 workers aboard, broke its heavy mooring cables in the midnight tempest and was blown out into the raging Florida straits.

50
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:35.680
[George] The barge broke apart in the mountainous waves, 82 men drowned in the sea, while dozens of survivors clung to floating wreckage for days before being picked up by passing steamships as far away as Key West and the Bahamas.

51
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:43.600
[George] Undaunted by the catastrophe, Flagler ordered construction redoubled, famously declaring, We must not stop.

52
00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.680
[George] We have put our hands to the plow, and we must finish the furrow.

53
00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:57.920
[George] Three years later, in October 1909, an even more ferocious Category 4 hurricane struck Marathon with winds exceeding 140 miles per hour.

54
00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:05.440
[George] The storm washed out miles of newly completed earthen embankments, demolished docks, and killed dozens of workers.

55
00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:09.760
[George] But Crome and his engineers learned vital lessons from each disaster.

56
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:22.480
[George] They abandoned vulnerable earthen fills in exposed deepwater channels, replacing them with indestructible reinforced concrete viaducts resting on massive granite piers sunk deep into the solid coral bedrock.

57
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:33.440
[George] Heroic rescue tugboats braved hurricane winds to pluck shivering workers from floating wreckage in the Gulf Stream, performing miraculous maritime rescues across international waters.

58
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:44.080
[George] To conquer the deepest ocean gaps, Flagler's engineers designed magnificent engineering masterpieces that pushed 20th century structural technology to its absolute limit.

59
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:58.880
[George] The crowning jewel was the monumental Long Key Viaduct, a graceful, sweeping concrete arch structure spanning 2.7 miles of open ocean, featuring 180 reinforced concrete arches rising 30 feet above the waves.

60
00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:08.680
[George] Even more formidable was the seven-mile bridge, stretching 6.79 miles across Moser Channel, connecting Knights Key to Little Duck Quay.

61
00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:22.120
[George] Constructed with 546 concrete piers sunk into the sea floor through caissons, the bridge combined 544 steel girder spans with a massive swing span to allow ocean vessels to pass.

62
00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:33.400
[George] Over 100,000 barrels of specialized German waterproof Portland cement were imported to construct the piers, ensuring the concrete would cure beneath saltwater without deteriorating.

63
00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:45.160
[George] At Baia Honda Key, where water depths reached 35 feet with vicious tidal currents, engineers constructed a soaring through truss steel bridge 13 feet above the water.

64
00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:58.120
[George] Creating a breathtaking visual spectacle where passengers aboard passing trains looked out both sides of their railcars into endless miles of turquoise sea, feeling as though they were flying across the surface of the ocean.

65
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:09.880
[George] The monumental concrete arches of the Long Key Viaduct were engineered to withstand the hydrodynamic sheer force of tidal waves, curring underwater into indestructible artificial stone.

66
00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:25.960
[George] By late 1911, after seven grueling years of construction and the expenditure of over $50 million of flagler's personal fortune, equivalent to more than 1.6 billion dollars today, the final spikes were driven into the ties at Key West.

67
00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.600
[George] The impossible railroad was complete.

68
00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:36.440
[George] On Monday morning, January 22, 1912, the official inaugural passenger train departed Miami.

69
00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:45.520
[George] Aboard his luxurious private railcar, the Rambler, was 82-year old Henry Flagler, accompanied by his third wife, Mary Lily Kennan.

70
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:52.960
[George] Flagler was frail, nearly blind, and walking with a cane, but his mind was sharp and radiant with joy.

71
00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:03.440
[George] As the special train rolled across the Long Key Viaduct and Seven Mile Bridge, passengers watched in awe as porpoises leaped in the crystal clear water alongside the tracks.

72
00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:18.560
[George] At 10.43 in the morning, the train rolled slowly into the Key West depot amidst the deafening roar of 30,000 cheering citizens, ringing church bells, blowing factory whistles, and cannon salutes from naval gunboats in the harbor.

73
00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:26.240
[George] A parade of two thousand schoolchildren dressed in white threw red carnations beneath Flagler's feet as he stepped onto the platform.

74
00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:33.760
[George] Surrounded by dignitaries, the elderly tycoon wept openly, whispering to a friend, Now I can die happy.

75
00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.680
[George] My dream is fulfilled.

76
00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:43.840
[George] Flagler passed away 16 months later, in May 1913, leaving behind an empire that transformed Florida forever.

77
00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:55.840
[George] Flagler's private railcar, equipped with hand-carved mahogany paneling and velvet draperies, served as the traveling command center for the greatest private engineering project in American history.

78
00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:02.880
[George] For 23 years, the oversea railroad operated as one of the premier luxury travel experiences on Earth.

79
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:17.200
[George] Passengers boarded the Havana Special at Pennsylvania Station in New York City and stepped off 38 hours later in Key West, transferring to luxury steamships that delivered them to the casinos and cabarets of Havana in just four hours.

80
00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:24.480
[George] Freight trains carried millions of tons of pineapples, cigars, sugar, and industrial goods across the sea.

81
00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:32.760
[George] However, the railroad's magnificent rain was brought to a tragic, violent end on Labor Day, September 2, 1935.

82
00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:41.320
[George] The monstrous 1935 Labor Day hurricane, the most intense tropical cyclone ever to strike the United States.

83
00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:51.160
[George] With sustained winds exceeding 200 miles per hour and a barometric pressure of 892 millibars, slammed directly into Islamorada.

84
00:12:51.160 --> 00:13:05.240
[George] A terrifying 17-foot storm surge swept across the tracks, overturning an 11-car rescue train and destroying 40 miles of earthen railway embankment, though the concrete viaducts survived virtually undamaged.

85
00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:11.640
[George] Struggling through the Great Depression, the Florida East Coast Railway could not afford to rebuild the tracks.

86
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:20.360
[George] In 1936, the railway right of way and bridges were purchased by the state of Florida for a modest $640,000.

87
00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:30.040
[George] State engineers paved over the indestructible concrete viaducts and steel spans, converting Flagler's railroad into the modern overseas highway, U.S.

88
00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:31.080
[George] Route 1.

89
00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:43.560
[George] Today, millions of travelers drive along the modern overseas highway, marveling at the preserved historic arches of the Long Key Viaduct and Seven Mile Bridge standing proudly alongside the highway.

90
00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:55.560
[George] Flagler's Oversea Railroad remains celebrated as the eighth wonder of the world, an eternal monument to the triumph of vision, perseverance, and engineering genius over the fury of the sea.

91
00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:07.400
[George] The historic concrete viaducts standing alongside the modern overseas highway remains celebrated worldwide as a masterwork of human perseverance, vision, and industrial craft.

92
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:20.240
[Rebecca] Henry Flagler's Oversea Railroad was hailed as the eighth wonder of the world, an extraordinary monument to human perseverance that conquered open ocean and devastating Caribbean hurricanes.

93
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:21.680
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

94
00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:36.640
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey deep into the Everglades for the story of Osceola and the unconquered Seminole Resistance.

